    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acral lentiginous melanoma of the right great toe nail unit, clinical stage IB (cT1b N0 M0) (C43.71)</content>. A thin acral melanoma of the nail unit, diagnosed nine days after he first came in, because a band that had not grown out and pigment on the nail fold were biopsied rather than called a bruise. Acral melanoma is the most common melanoma in people with darker skin and is not caused by the sun. At 0.8 mm it is T1b: no scans or blood tests are needed, and a sentinel lymph node biopsy is offered because the chance of a positive node is about 5 to 10 percent. Dr. Mbeki-Rowe set out the two surgical choices: amputation of the toe at the interphalangeal joint, or digit-sparing excision of the whole nail unit down to the bone with a skin graft. For a melanoma this thin, digit-sparing excision is an accepted choice with similar survival in published series, at the cost of a higher chance of a local recurrence that would then need amputation. With his values in view, he chose digit-sparing excision with a sentinel node biopsy, and agreed in advance that amputation would be the next step if the margins were involved or the melanoma proved thicker. [S]<list><item>Digit-sparing excision of the nail unit with a full-thickness skin graft and right inguinal sentinel node biopsy with Dr. Mbeki-Rowe in two weeks, after lymphoscintigraphy the same morning.</item><item>No staging scans or LDH.</item><item>No blood tests needed before surgery for a healthy man of 32.</item><item>A written summary of the decision given to him.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Dark band in the right great toenail with pigment on the nail fold (L60.8)</content>. Explained by the biopsy; superseded. [S]<list><item>Problem resolved.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Biopsy pathology: Breslow thickness, ulceration, mitotic rate, margins, and microsatellites</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>0.8 mm, not ulcerated, mitotic rate 1 per mm2, no lymphovascular or perineural invasion, no microsatellites; melanoma in situ at the lateral edges</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Dermoscopy of the nail</td><td>At presentation with a pigmented nail band</td><td>Up to date</td><td>By the dermatologist two days after the first visit: irregular lines and pigment on the nail fold</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Full skin and nail examination, including the scalp, palms, soles, between the toes, and all nails</td><td>At diagnosis, then at each surveillance visit</td><td>Deferred</td><td>A deviation: the dermatologist examined the hands, feet and nails at the biopsy visit, but a full skin examination at diagnosis was not recorded. Dr. Clarke examined the whole skin today and found no other suspicious lesion, so no harm resulted. Corrective action: a yearly full skin examination by dermatology is booked, and the full examination is part of each surveillance visit</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Examination of the lymph node basins (right popliteal and inguinal) and the scar</td><td>At diagnosis, then at each surveillance visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No palpable nodes</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Imaging (CT, PET, or ultrasound) and LDH</td><td>Not routine for stage I to II without symptoms</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Clinical stage IB without symptoms; scans and LDH would not change management</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Sentinel lymph node biopsy</td><td>Discussed and offered for T1b melanoma</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Accepted; with the excision in two weeks, after lymphoscintigraphy</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Surgical oncology</td><td>At diagnosis of invasive melanoma</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen this morning; recommendations in the consultation note</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Shared decision on the extent of surgery</td><td>Before definitive surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Amputation and digit-sparing excision explained with their outcomes; he chose digit-sparing excision; written summary given</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Germline genetic testing</td><td>Only with several primary melanomas or a strong family history of melanoma or pancreatic cancer</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>A single primary melanoma; no criterion met</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Distress and depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 4 and GAD-7 6; no thoughts of self-harm</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Tissue diagnosis within two weeks of presentation. Outcome by 1977-06-17: Dermoscopy and nail matrix biopsy two days after presentation; diagnosis explained on the ninth day [S]</item><item>Definitive surgery with clear margins and lymph node staging within four weeks of diagnosis, by the approach he chose. In progress; target 1977-07-15 [S]</item><item>Walk, run, and play football normally within four months of surgery. In progress; target 1977-10-19 [S]</item><item>Monthly skin and nail self-examination, including the palms, soles, between the toes and all nails. In progress; target 1977-10-19 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Melanoma: Cutaneous, Version 3.2026 (2026); Swetter SM, et al. Guidelines of care for the management of primary cutaneous melanoma. J Am Acad Dermatol 2019;80(1):208-250 (2019); Gershenwald JE, et al. Melanoma staging: Evidence-based changes in the American Joint Committee on Cancer eighth edition cancer staging manual. CA Cancer J Clin 2017;67(6):472-492 (2017); Wong SL, et al. Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy and Management of Regional Lymph Nodes in Melanoma: American Society of Clinical Oncology and Society of Surgical Oncology Clinical Practice Guideline Update. J Clin Oncol 2018;36(4):399-413 (2018); Chouquet L, et al. Reconstruction After Wide Excision of the Nail Apparatus in the Treatment of Melanoma: A Systematic Literature Review. J Clin Med 2025;14(17):5932 (2025); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Surgery in two weeks with Dr. Mbeki-Rowe. Review two weeks after surgery with the final pathology. Call for a lump in the groin, or for redness or discharge at the biopsy site. Next scheduled visit 1977-07-15: review two weeks after digit-sparing excision and sentinel node biopsy: final pathology and stage, graft care, and the surveillance plan. [S]</paragraph></text>

